WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A useful log entry is clear, consistent, and searchable.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.200
Timestamps should be precise and time-zone aware.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.020
Event types should describe what happened in a stable way.

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.640
Severity should reflect operational and security meaning rather than personal preference.

5
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:26.660
Actor identity, source context, resource identifiers, action, outcome, and correlation ID often matter more than a long free-form message.

6
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:35.400
Logs should help a responder answer the basic questions: who, what, when, where, against which resource, and with what result.

7
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.880
Structured logging makes that possible by using consistent fields that tools can parse.

8
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:57.380
Instead of burying everything in one text string, a structured log can include fields such as event_type, actor_id, tenant_id, resource_type, resource_id, action, outcome, client_ip, request_id, service, and severity.

9
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.840
The exact fields depend on the system, but consistency across services is the key.

10
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:10.620
Consistent fields let teams search, aggregate, alert, and build timelines without fragile text matching.

11
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.100
Log quality also depends on context and restraint.

12
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.840
Too little detail makes logs useless.

13
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:22.300
Too much detail creates noise, cost, and privacy risk.

14
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:30.480
Use correlation IDs to connect user-facing errors, API calls, background jobs, and downstream service requests.

15
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:36.420
Avoid ambiguous messages like failed or error without a useful event type or outcome.

16
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:43.480
Make sure normal operations, expected rejections, warning conditions, and critical failures are distinguishable.

17
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:49.100
Good logs are written for future responders who will not have the original developer sitting beside them.
